Part II — The Formal Problem

2.1 Partially Observable Markov Decision Processes

We model the human–AI dialogue as a POMDP. A POMDP is a tuple:

\langle S, A, O, T, \Omega, R, \gamma \rangle

where S is the set of latent states, A is the set of actions, O is the set of observations, T(s' \mid s, a) is the transition probability, \Omega(o \mid s', a) is the observation probability, R(s, a) is the reward function, and \gamma \in [0,1) is the discount factor.

The AI does not observe s directly. In the standard POMDP formulation, it maintains a belief state b \in \Delta(S), updated via Bayes:

b'(s') = \frac{\Omega(o \mid s', a) \sum_{s \in S} T(s' \mid s, a) b(s)}{\Pr(o \mid b, a)}

The belief state is a sufficient statistic for optimal control: optimal control depends on b, not on the raw history. Using policies that rely on the entire data history does not provide any performance improvement over policies that depend only on the belief state.

2.2 Latent Variable: Wellbeing Support Need

We do not attempt to model clinical state. Instead:

Z_t \in \{0 = \text{no check warranted}, \; 1 = \text{check-in useful}, \; 2 = \text{stronger support warranted}\}

This is a response policy variable, not a psychiatric classifier. The AI's task is to choose actions, not diagnoses.

This distinction is critical. Mental-R1 (Wang et al., 2026) trains LLM reasoning for mental-health assessment, demonstrating clear advantages on reasoning-intensive cases. Their task is classification: given a user's text, predict a mental-health label. Our task is decision: given incomplete observations, select a response under asymmetric loss. The distinction:

\boxed{
\text{mental-health assessment} \neq \text{wellbeing-support decision under latent-state uncertainty}
}

2.3 The Observation Model

X_t \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid Z_t, a_{t-1})

The AI observes linguistic patterns, project metadata, and interaction history. It does not observe Z_t directly.

