Part III — Non-Identification Under Insufficiently Constrained Overlapping Models

3.1 The Identifiability Problem

A fundamental problem in statistics and causal inference is that of identifiability: whether the parameters of interest can be uniquely determined from the available data. The partial identification literature formalizes the case where data plus assumptions identify a set rather than a unique parameter. Recent developments in partial identification provide inference methods for the full parameter vector or subvectors, establishing efficiency bounds and consistency properties.

Definition 1 (Overlapping Observation Models). Two latent hypotheses H_i and H_j have overlapping observation models given observation space \mathcal{O} if there exists O \in \mathcal{O} such that:

\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(O \mid H_j) > 0

Critical distinction. Support overlap is a necessary condition for non-identification to be possible. It is not sufficient. If the likelihoods \Pr(O \mid H_i) and priors \Pr(H_i) are fully specified, the posterior is perfectly identified:

\Pr(H_i \mid O) = \frac{\Pr(O \mid H_i) \Pr(H_i)}{\sum_j \Pr(O \mid H_j) \Pr(H_j)}

Non-identification arises from the insufficient constraints on the likelihoods and priors, not from the overlap itself.

3.2 Theorem 1 — The Corrected Statement

Theorem 1 (Non-Identification Under Insufficiently Constrained Overlapping Models).

Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses and O the observed conversational data. Suppose at least two hypotheses assign positive probability to O:

\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(O \mid H_j) > 0 \quad (i \neq j)

If the likelihood ratios \Pr(O \mid H_i) / \Pr(O \mid H_j) and/or the prior probabilities \Pr(H_i) required to determine \Pr(H_i \mid O) are not identified by the available data and assumptions, then \Pr(H_i \mid O) is not point-identified.

The identified posterior set is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{P}_i(O) = \left\{ \Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O) \right\}
}

where \mathcal{M}(O) is the set of observational models compatible with the available evidence.

The bounds are:

p_L(O) = \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O), \quad p_U(O) = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O)

so that:

\boxed{
\Pr(H_i \mid O) \in [p_L(O), p_U(O)]
}

Proof. The posterior is a function of the likelihoods and priors:

\Pr(H_i \mid O) = \frac{\Pr(O \mid H_i) \Pr(H_i)}{\sum_j \Pr(O \mid H_j) \Pr(H_j)}

If the available data and assumptions do not determine the likelihood ratios or the priors, then there exists a set \mathcal{M}(O) of observation models—each compatible with the evidence—that induce different posteriors. The posterior is therefore a set-valued functional of the evidence. The identified set is \mathcal{P}_i(O) = \{\Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O)\}. \square

3.3 The Identified-Model Set

Define the contextual information set at time t:

\mathcal{I}_t = \{X_1, \ldots, X_t, a_1, \ldots, a_{t-1}\}

The compatible model set is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{M}_t = \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{I}_t) = \left\{ P : P \text{ is consistent with } \mathcal{I}_t \right\}
}

The identified posterior set for Z is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{P}_t(Z) = \left\{ P(Z \mid \mathcal{I}_t) : P \in \mathcal{M}_t \right\}
}

The uncertainty width is:

\boxed{
W_t = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t) - \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t)
}

Important nuance. More information narrows the identified set only under appropriate identification conditions. Adding observations does not automatically reduce W_t. The contraction:

\mathcal{M}_{t+1} \subseteq \mathcal{M}_t

is an empirical hypothesis, not an assumption.

3.4 The Four Hypotheses

· H_1: Wellbeing concern requiring check
· H_2: Unusual but healthy intellectual workflow
· H_3: AI-amplified research productivity
· H_4: Observation artifact (memory selection bias)

Each can generate the same observation pattern O = \{\text{high breadth} + \text{high pace} + \text{limited observed external validation}\} with positive probability. Therefore O is non-identifying.

3.5 Missing Context as Partial Identification

Let L = complete user life, M \subset L = model-accessible information. Then:

\neg \text{Observe}(I) \not\Rightarrow \neg \text{Exists}(I)

The absence of an observation is not evidence of absence. This is the open-world assumption problem: the AI's memory is not a complete representation of the user's life.

Note on formalization. A naive contextual completeness ratio C = |M| / |L| is mathematically indefensible because |L|—the cardinality of a complete human life—is not measurable. Instead, we operationalize contextual information through the compatible model set \mathcal{M}_t and the uncertainty width W_t.

