Part IV — Decision Under Asymmetric Loss

4.1 The Bayes-Optimal Decision Rule

Given belief set \mathcal{M}_t, the AI selects:

a^* = \arg\min_{a \in A} \mathbb{E}[L(a, Z) \mid \mathcal{M}_t]

with A = \{a_{\text{ignore}}, a_{\text{ask}}, a_{\text{escalate}}\}.

4.2 Asymmetric Loss Matrix

L = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} & C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \\
C_{\text{query}} & 0 & C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ask}} \\
C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} & C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} & 0
\end{pmatrix}

Crisis carve-out. If C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \to \infty (imminent risk), the optimal threshold for intervention approaches zero. However, this applies only when there is specific evidence of imminent risk (E0-level statements), not merely unusual productivity. The relevant question is whether the evidence is sufficient to establish imminent risk. If the AI cannot distinguish imminent risk from benign unusual behavior, it faces the same non-identifiability problem.

