Part V — The Wellbeing Query as Active Information Gathering

5.1 Value of Information

The query action a_q is an information-gathering action. Its value:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) = \underbrace{\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)]}_{\text{current optimal worst-case loss}} - \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]}_{\text{expected future worst-case loss}}

where \mathcal{M}_{t+1} is the compatible model set after observing the user's response to the query.

Theorem 2 (Value-of-Information Criterion for Wellbeing Queries). Given belief set \mathcal{M}_t, query action a_q, query cost C_q, and optimal post-query policy, the query is preferred whenever:

\boxed{VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) > C_q}

Proof. Compare expected losses:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] = C_q + \mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]

\mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] = C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)

Query is optimal when \mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] < \mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] and \mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] < \mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{escalate}})]. The first inequality gives the VOI criterion. \square

Interpretation. The AI asks because information has decision value, not because it has diagnosed the user. The value of information is an expected value: the expected value after a test minus the expected value without the test. Information collection reduces uncertainty, which makes us choose safer solutions as insurance. Inserting an information-gathering stage before a decision problem is a key strategy.

5.2 The Interactive Calibration Bridge

Li et al. (ACL 2026) establish that calibration error in LLM density estimation admits a non-vanishing lower bound under non-interactive learning, while interaction empirically reduces it. They further characterize that calibration error identifies informative queries and that calibration can be accelerated by shifting query distributions from imbalanced to balanced regimes. Their proposed calibration-driven Interactive Learning Strategy (ILS) selects clarification queries by optimizing calibration error, providing both theoretical guarantees and empirical gains.

This gives us a clean conceptual chain:

\boxed{
\text{Non-interactive calibration limit} \rightarrow \text{information deficit} \rightarrow \text{interaction} \rightarrow \text{uncertainty reduction}
}

The distinction. Their target is concept calibration—the model's confidence in factual or conceptual predictions. Our target is latent human-support state inference—the model's belief about an unobserved wellbeing state. The ACL result motivates the VOI criterion but does not transfer directly. The mapping from concept calibration to latent-state inference is not established.

Our open question:

\boxed{
\text{Does the calibration floor for latent wellbeing-state inference have a different structure than the concept calibration floor?}
}

5.3 Special Case: Binary Threshold

If the query provides no information, then VOI = 0 and the criterion reduces to the standard binary comparison:

\text{Escalate if } p > \frac{C_{\text{FP}}}{C_{\text{FP}} + C_{\text{FN}}}, \quad \text{Ignore otherwise}

This is a special case, not the general result.

5.4 The Empirical Hypothesis

H1 (Uncertainty Contraction). For appropriately selected informative queries:

\Delta W_t = W_t - W_{t+1} > 0

That is, the uncertainty width contracts after the query. This is testable, not assumed.

5.5 The Empirical Prediction

\boxed{
\text{Informative interaction} \Rightarrow \text{uncertainty contraction} \Rightarrow \text{better decision calibration}
}

We measure:

· W_t before a query;
· W_{t+1} after a query;
· \Delta W_t = W_t - W_{t+1};
· VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t);
· Decision quality under R_{\text{standard}} vs. R_{\text{calibrated}}.

If \Delta W_t > 0 consistently, the query is genuinely informative. If \Delta W_t \approx 0, the query provides no decision value.

