Part VI — Robustness to Misclassification

6.1 The Robust Decision Rule

A naive system optimizes:

\min_a \mathbb{E}[L(a, Z) \mid \hat{b}]

where \hat{b} is the estimated belief. A robust system instead recognizes uncertainty over the inference model:

\boxed{a_t^* = \arg\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z) \mid \mathcal{I}_t]}

This is the robust Bayes decision rule over the identified set. Giacomini & Kitagawa (2021) reconcile the asymptotic disagreement between Bayesian and frequentist inference in set-identified models by adopting a multiple-prior (robust) Bayesian approach. They construct a prior class that removes the need to specify an unrevisable prior for the structural parameter, and summarize the resulting class of posteriors by reporting posterior lower and upper probabilities and robust credible regions. Aradillas Fernández et al. (2024) study binary treatment choice problems with partial identification through the lens of robust (multiple prior) Bayesian analysis, deriving optimal rules under uncertainty.

6.2 The Robust Misclassification Principle

When Z is not point-identified from available observations, the response policy should be evaluated not only under the most likely state estimate but across the identified set of plausible latent-state models. This protects against the consequences of \hat{Z} \neq Z.

\boxed{
\text{Alignment requires robustness to user-state misclassification.}
}

This is broader than mental-health interaction. The same principle applies to grief, anger, enthusiasm, political beliefs, unusual creativity, scientific speculation, interpersonal conflict, risk-taking, and existential discussions.

