Part VII — Calibration: Imported Theory and Open Problem

7.1 Imported Result (Not Our Theorem)

Kalai & Vempala (STOC 2024) prove that for "arbitrary" facts whose veracity cannot be determined from training data, hallucinations must occur at a rate close to the fraction of facts occurring exactly once (the Good–Turing monofact estimate), for language models satisfying a statistical calibration condition. The bound is:

\mathrm{Cal} \geq \mathrm{MFR} - \mathrm{Ale} - \frac{3e^{-m}}{\delta} - \sqrt{\frac{6\ln(6/\delta)}{N}}

Miao & Kearns (PNAS 2026) empirically investigate this relationship, finding that hallucination in large language models can be controlled through deliberate manipulation of training data frequency distributions. They validate the theory by examining how different underlying data distributions affect the monofact rate and hallucination rates.

Critical distinction. This theorem concerns calibration of factual prediction under a training-data distribution. It does not automatically transfer to calibration of latent-user-state inference. The mapping from language-model calibration to conversational state inference is not established.

7.2 Our Open Research Question

\boxed{
\text{Does calibration uncertainty in conversational state inference admit a lower bound under partial observability?}
}

Define the user-state calibration error:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left| P_\theta(Z = 1 \mid X) - P(Z = 1 \mid X) \right| \right]

Open problem. Is there a non-vanishing lower bound on \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} determined by:

· incomplete context (\mathcal{M}_t large);
· hypothesis overlap (\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 for multiple i);
· baseline uncertainty;
· observation noise;
· insufficient interactive information?

This is a new theoretical problem, not a borrowed theorem.

7.3 Proper Scoring Rules

A proper scoring rule S(p, y) satisfies:

\mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(p, Y)] \leq \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(q, Y)] \quad \forall q

The expected loss decomposes:

\mathbb{E}[S(p, Y)] = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(p, \bar{p})]}_{\text{calibration}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(\bar{p}, Y)]}_{\text{refinement}}

where \bar{p} = \Pr(Y = 1 \mid p). Calibration measures whether predicted probabilities match empirical frequencies; refinement measures informativeness. DeGroot and Fienberg showed that these two terms make up proper scoring rules in general, and that probabilistic predictions should minimize refinement loss subject to calibration.

For wellbeing decisions, both matter: the AI needs informative predictions (to distinguish Z = 1 from Z = 0) and calibrated predictions (to set thresholds correctly).

