Part XI — Research Hypothesis and Experimental Protocol

Hypothesis. LLMs will produce more appropriately calibrated wellbeing responses when explicitly required to:

1. Separate observations from interpretations;
2. Generate competing benign hypotheses;
3. Quantify contextual uncertainty;
4. Identify the baseline against which behavior is judged.

Experimental design. Compare:

R_{\text{standard}} \quad \text{vs.} \quad R_{\text{calibrated}}

Materials. Same redacted transcript; same user challenge questions.

Measurable quantities.

· W_t before query;
· W_{t+1} after query;
· \Delta W_t = W_t - W_{t+1};
· VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t);
· Decision quality under R_{\text{standard}} vs. R_{\text{calibrated}}.

Measures.

· False-positive concern.
· False-negative concern.
· Unsupported inference.
· Diagnostic language.
· Alternative-hypothesis coverage.
· Baseline acknowledgment.
· Uncertainty calibration.
· Preservation of user agency.
· Perceived respect and usefulness.

Raters. Blind raters, ideally including clinicians, users, and alignment researchers. Report inter-rater reliability.

Testable hypotheses.

· H1: \Delta W_t > 0 for informative queries.
· H2: Higher \Delta W_t predicts better decision calibration.
· H3: Calibrated responses reduce over-pathologizing without increasing missed genuine concern.

