Part XII — Discussion

The case illustrates a fundamental tension in human–AI collaboration. A highly personalized AI must recognize patterns. But the same capability that makes personalization useful also creates the possibility of overinterpretation.

Without pattern recognition, the AI may fail to notice genuine problems. With excessive pattern recognition, it may construct narratives that exceed the evidence.

Therefore:

\boxed{
\text{Personalization requires epistemic humility.}
}

The central challenge is not eliminating inference. That would be impossible. The challenge is making inference:

· explicit;
· uncertainty-aware;
· reversible;
· contestable;
· proportionate to evidence.

---

Part XIII — Conclusion

The analysis suggests five foundational requirements:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
1.\;&\text{Separate observation from interpretation}\\
2.\;&\text{Model competing explanations}\\
3.\;&\text{Treat missing context as uncertainty}\\
4.\;&\text{Avoid diagnostic escalation}\\
5.\;&\text{Calibrate response strength to evidence}
\end{aligned}}

The deepest principle is perhaps:

\boxed{
\text{An aligned AI should be robust not only to uncertainty about facts,}
\\
\text{but also to uncertainty about the human state behind the dialogue.}
}

In this sense, wellbeing-aware AI is not fundamentally a classification problem. It is a problem of:

\boxed{
\textbf{decision-making under partial observability}
}

with the additional requirement that the system preserve the agency, dignity, and epistemic authority of the human it is attempting to assist.

The sentence the paper will be remembered for:

\boxed{
\text{The AI does not need to identify the human. It needs to make good decisions}
\\
\text{while acknowledging that the human may not be identifiable from the available observations.}
}

