APPENDICES

Inference Under Partial Observability

Appendix A — POMDP Background and Belief-State Sufficiency

A.1 Definition of a POMDP

A partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) is a tuple \langle S, A, O, T, \Omega, R, \gamma \rangle where:

· S is a finite set of latent states.
· A is a finite set of actions.
· O is a finite set of observations.
· T(s' \mid s, a) = \Pr(S_{t+1} = s' \mid S_t = s, A_t = a) is the transition probability.
· \Omega(o \mid s', a) = \Pr(O_{t+1} = o \mid S_{t+1} = s', A_t = a) is the observation probability.
· R: S \times A \to \mathbb{R} is the reward function.
· \gamma \in [0,1) is the discount factor.

A.2 Belief State

The AI does not observe S_t directly. It maintains a belief state b_t \in \Delta(S), a probability distribution over latent states:

b_t(s) = \Pr(S_t = s \mid X_{1:t}, A_{1:t-1})

A.3 Belief Update (Bayes' Rule)

Upon observing o_{t+1} after taking action a_t, the belief is updated:

b_{t+1}(s') = \frac{\Omega(o_{t+1} \mid s', a_t) \sum_{s \in S} T(s' \mid s, a_t) b_t(s)}{\Pr(o_{t+1} \mid b_t, a_t)}

where the denominator is the normalizing constant:

\Pr(o_{t+1} \mid b_t, a_t) = \sum_{s' \in S} \Omega(o_{t+1} \mid s', a_t) \sum_{s \in S} T(s' \mid s, a_t) b_t(s)

A.4 Belief-State Sufficiency

Theorem A.1 (Belief-State Sufficiency). In a POMDP, the belief state b_t is a sufficient statistic for the interaction history h_t = (X_{1:t}, A_{1:t-1}) with respect to optimal control. That is, the optimal policy depends on h_t only through b_t.

Proof. The belief state is a sufficient statistic if the distribution of future observations and rewards depends on the history only through the belief. Formally, for any history h_t and any future trajectory \tau = (o_{t+1}, r_{t+1}, o_{t+2}, r_{t+2}, \ldots):

\Pr(\tau \mid h_t, a_t) = \Pr(\tau \mid b_t, a_t)

The belief update is Markovian: b_{t+1} depends only on b_t, a_t, and o_{t+1}. The expected reward at time t depends only on b_t and a_t:

\mathbb{E}[R(S_t, a_t) \mid h_t] = \sum_{s \in S} R(s, a_t) b_t(s) = \mathbb{E}[R(S_t, a_t) \mid b_t]

By induction, the distribution of all future observations and rewards depends on h_t only through b_t. Therefore, the belief state is a sufficient statistic for optimal control. \square

Remark A.1. This is the classical result of POMDP theory. Using policies that rely on the entire data history does not provide any performance improvement over policies that depend only on the belief state. The belief state assigns probability to every process state and reflects the extent to which states are believed to be present.

A.5 Optimal Policy

The optimal policy \pi^*: \Delta(S) \to A satisfies the Bellman equation:

V^*(b) = \max_{a \in A} \left[ \sum_{s \in S} b(s) R(s, a) + \gamma \sum_{o \in O} \Pr(o \mid b, a) V^*(b') \right]

where b' is the updated belief after observing o and taking action a.

