Appendix B — Proof of Theorem 1 (Non-Identification Under Insufficiently Constrained Overlapping Models)

B.1 Formal Setup

Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses (e.g., H_1: wellbeing concern; H_2: healthy workflow; H_3: AI-amplified productivity; H_4: observation artifact).

Let O \in \mathcal{O} be an observed conversational data pattern.

Let \mathcal{M}(O) be the set of observational models compatible with the available evidence:

\mathcal{M}(O) = \left\{ P : P \text{ is consistent with } O \text{ and the available assumptions} \right\}

B.2 Definitions

Definition B.1 (Overlapping Observation Models). Two hypotheses H_i and H_j have overlapping observation models for observation O if:

\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(O \mid H_j) > 0

Definition B.2 (Identified Posterior Set). The identified posterior set for H_i given O is:

\mathcal{P}_i(O) = \left\{ \Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O) \right\}

B.3 Theorem Statement

Theorem 1 (Non-Identification Under Insufficiently Constrained Overlapping Models).

Let H_1, \ldots, H_k be latent hypotheses and O the observed conversational data. Suppose at least two hypotheses assign positive probability to O:

\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(O \mid H_j) > 0 \quad (i \neq j)

If the likelihood ratios \Pr(O \mid H_i) / \Pr(O \mid H_j) and/or the prior probabilities \Pr(H_i) required to determine \Pr(H_i \mid O) are not identified by the available data and assumptions, then \Pr(H_i \mid O) is not point-identified.

The identified posterior set is:

\mathcal{P}_i(O) = \left\{ \Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O) \right\}

with bounds:

p_L(O) = \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O), \quad p_U(O) = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}(O)} \Pr(H_i \mid O)

so that:

\Pr(H_i \mid O) \in [p_L(O), p_U(O)]

B.4 Proof

The posterior probability is given by Bayes' rule:

\Pr(H_i \mid O) = \frac{\Pr(O \mid H_i) \Pr(H_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^k \Pr(O \mid H_j) \Pr(H_j)}

This posterior is a function of:

1. The likelihoods \Pr(O \mid H_j) for all j.
2. The priors \Pr(H_j) for all j.

If the available data and assumptions do not determine the likelihood ratios or the priors, then there exists a set \mathcal{M}(O) of observation models—each compatible with the evidence—that induce different posteriors.

Specifically, for any two models P, P' \in \mathcal{M}(O), if:

\frac{P(O \mid H_i)}{P(O \mid H_j)} \neq \frac{P'(O \mid H_i)}{P'(O \mid H_j)}

or:

P(H_i) \neq P'(H_i)

then:

P(H_i \mid O) \neq P'(H_i \mid O)

Therefore, the posterior is a set-valued functional of the evidence:

\mathcal{P}_i(O) = \left\{ \Pr(H_i \mid O) : P \in \mathcal{M}(O) \right\}

The identified set is non-singleton (p_L < p_U) whenever there exist two compatible models that induce different posteriors. \square

B.5 Corollaries

Corollary B.1. The statement "the pattern is worth checking" is epistemically valid. The statement "the pattern indicates a wellbeing concern" is not, because it requires collapsing non-identifiable hypotheses into a single diagnostic conclusion.

Corollary B.2. When contextual information is incomplete, the set \mathcal{M}(O) is larger, and the identified set [p_L, p_U] is potentially wider. This relationship is an empirical hypothesis, not an assumption.

Corollary B.3 (The Four Hypotheses). For the observation pattern O = \{\text{high breadth} + \text{high pace} + \text{limited observed external validation}\}, each of the four hypotheses H_1–H_4 assigns positive probability. Therefore, O is non-identifying.

