Appendix C — Proof of Theorem 2 (Value-of-Information Criterion for Wellbeing Queries)

C.1 Formal Setup

Let \mathcal{M}_t be the compatible model set at time t.

Let a_q be a query action (wellbeing check).

Let C_q be the query cost.

Let \mathcal{M}_{t+1} be the compatible model set after observing the user's response o \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q).

C.2 Definitions

Definition C.1 (Value of Information). The value of information of query a_q given model set \mathcal{M}_t is:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) = \underbrace{\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)]}_{\text{current optimal worst-case loss}} - \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]}_{\text{expected future worst-case loss}}

C.3 Theorem Statement

Theorem 2 (Value-of-Information Criterion for Wellbeing Queries).

Given model set \mathcal{M}_t, query action a_q, query cost C_q, and optimal post-query policy, the query is preferred whenever:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) > C_q

C.4 Proof

Compare the expected losses of the available actions:

Ignore action:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] = C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)

Escalate action:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{escalate}})] = C_{\text{FP}}^{\text{escalate}} \cdot \left(1 - \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)\right)

Query action:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] = C_q + \mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]

The query is optimal when:

\mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] < \mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{ignore}})] \quad \text{and} \quad \mathbb{E}[L(a_q)] < \mathbb{E}[L(a_{\text{escalate}})]

The first inequality gives:

C_q + \mathbb{E}_{o} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right] < C_{\text{FN}}^{\text{ignore}} \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1)

Rearranging:

\min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] - \mathbb{E}_{o} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right] > C_q

The left-hand side is VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t). Therefore:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) > C_q

The second inequality is satisfied whenever the query reduces the worst-case loss sufficiently. \square

C.5 Special Case: Binary Threshold

If the query provides no information (i.e., \Omega(o \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q) is independent of Z), then \mathcal{M}_{t+1} = \mathcal{M}_t and VOI = 0. The criterion reduces to:

\text{Escalate if } p > \frac{C_{\text{FP}}}{C_{\text{FP}} + C_{\text{FN}}}, \quad \text{Ignore otherwise}

where p = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1). This is a special case, not the general result.

C.6 Interpretation

The AI asks because information has decision value, not because it has diagnosed the user. The value of information is an expected value: the expected value after a test minus the expected value without the test. Information collection reduces uncertainty, which makes us choose safer solutions as insurance.

