Appendix E — Calibration: Imported Theory and Open Problem

E.1 The Kalai–Vempala Theorem (STOC 2024)

Kalai and Vempala (2024) show that there is an inherent statistical lower-bound on the rate that pretrained language models hallucinate certain types of facts. For "arbitrary" facts whose veracity cannot be determined from the training data, hallucinations must occur at a certain rate for language models that satisfy a statistical calibration condition appropriate for generative language models.

Specifically, if the maximum probability of any fact is bounded, the probability of generating a hallucination is close to the fraction of facts that occur exactly once in the training data (a "Good–Turing" estimate), even assuming ideal training data without errors.

The bound is:

\mathrm{Cal} \geq \mathrm{MFR} - \mathrm{Ale} - \frac{3e^{-m}}{\delta} - \sqrt{\frac{6\ln(6/\delta)}{N}}

where:

· \mathrm{MFR} is the monofact rate (fraction of facts appearing exactly once).
· \mathrm{Ale} is aleatoric uncertainty.
· m is a sparsity parameter.
· N is the training set size.

One conclusion is that models pretrained to be sufficiently good predictors (i.e., calibrated) may require post-training to mitigate hallucinations on the type of arbitrary facts that tend to appear once in the training set.

E.2 The Miao–Kearns Empirical Investigation (PNAS 2026)

Miao and Kearns (2026) empirically investigate the relationship between monofacts and miscalibration. They validate the theory by examining how different underlying data distributions affect the monofact rate and hallucination rates. They find that hallucination in large language models can be controlled through deliberate manipulation of training data frequency distributions.

E.3 The Li et al. Interactive Calibration Result (ACL 2026)

Li et al. (2026) model the uncertainty of LLMs in interactive settings and uncover the mechanism of active calibration between model concepts and human evaluations. They show that:

1. Calibration error in LLMs density estimation admits a non-vanishing lower bound under non-interactive learning.
2. Interaction empirically reduces this calibration error.
3. Calibration error identifies informative queries.
4. Calibration can be accelerated by shifting query distributions from imbalanced to balanced regimes.

They propose a calibration-driven Interactive Learning Strategy (ILS) that selects clarification queries by optimizing calibration error, providing both theoretical guarantees and empirical gains for reliability.

E.4 The Bridge and the Distinction

This gives a clean conceptual chain:

\boxed{
\text{Non-interactive calibration limit} \rightarrow \text{information deficit} \rightarrow \text{interaction} \rightarrow \text{uncertainty reduction}
}

The distinction. The target of Kalai–Vempala and Li et al. is concept calibration—the model's confidence in factual or conceptual predictions. Our target is latent human-support state inference—the model's belief about an unobserved wellbeing state. The ACL result motivates the VOI criterion but does not transfer directly. The mapping from concept calibration to latent-state inference is not established.

E.5 Our Open Research Question

\boxed{
\text{Does calibration uncertainty in conversational state inference admit a lower bound under partial observability?}
}

Define the user-state calibration error:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left| P_\theta(Z = 1 \mid X) - P(Z = 1 \mid X) \right| \right]

Open problem. Is there a non-vanishing lower bound on \mathcal{C}_{\text{user}} determined by:

· incomplete context (\mathcal{M}_t large);
· hypothesis overlap (\Pr(O \mid H_i) > 0 for multiple i);
· baseline uncertainty;
· observation noise;
· insufficient interactive information?

This is a new theoretical problem, not a borrowed theorem.

E.6 Proper Scoring Rules

A proper scoring rule S(p, y) satisfies:

\mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(p, Y)] \leq \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p}[S(q, Y)] \quad \forall q

The expected loss decomposes:

\mathbb{E}[S(p, Y)] = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(p, \bar{p})]}_{\text{calibration}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[S(\bar{p}, Y)]}_{\text{refinement}}

where \bar{p} = \Pr(Y = 1 \mid p). DeGroot and Fienberg showed that these two terms make up proper scoring rules in general, and that probabilistic predictions should minimize refinement loss subject to calibration.

