Appendix F — Value of Information: Detailed Formulation

F.1 General VOI

The value of information of query a_q given model set \mathcal{M}_t is:

VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t) = \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] - \mathbb{E}_{o \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_t, a_q)} \left[ \min_a \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_{t+1}} \mathbb{E}_P[L(a, Z)] \right]

F.2 Empirical Hypothesis H1

H1 (Uncertainty Contraction). For appropriately selected informative queries:

\Delta W_t = W_t - W_{t+1} > 0

where:

W_t = \sup_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t) - \inf_{P \in \mathcal{M}_t} P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{I}_t)

F.3 Empirical Prediction

\boxed{
\text{Informative interaction} \Rightarrow \text{uncertainty contraction} \Rightarrow \text{better decision calibration}
}

F.4 Measurable Quantities

· W_t before a query.
· W_{t+1} after a query.
· \Delta W_t = W_t - W_{t+1}.
· VOI(a_q \mid \mathcal{M}_t).
· Decision quality under R_{\text{standard}} vs. R_{\text{calibrated}}.

F.5 Testable Hypotheses

· H1: \Delta W_t > 0 for informative queries.
· H2: Higher \Delta W_t predicts better decision calibration.
· H3: Calibrated responses reduce over-pathologizing without increasing missed genuine concern.

